**Safety parts we’ll add for a “Delete my account” button**

| Safety part | What it does | Why it matters |
|-------------|--------------|----------------|
| **Confirmation step** | The user must click “Yes, delete my account” after reading a brief warning. | Prevents accidental deletion. |
| **Server‑side deletion** | The delete request goes to a secure API route on your server; the Supabase service key stays on the server. | Keeps the key hidden from the browser. |
| **Delete only the user’s own data** | The server checks the logged‑in user’s ID before removing rows and files. | Nobody can delete someone else’s stuff. |
| **Anonymise / keep minimal data** | Rows that need to stay for legal / compliance reasons (e.g. invoices) are marked as *public* or stored in a separate safe table. | Meets privacy laws. |
| **Remove associated storage files** | Any files the user uploaded (profile picture, docs, etc.) are deleted from the Supabase bucket. | Keeps the bucket tidy and private. |
| **Cleanup after deletion** | The user’s authentication record is removed from Supabase Auth. | They can’t log in again. |
| **Message and redirect** | Show a friendly “Your account has been deleted” page and redirect to the homepage. | Good UX and confirmation. |

